            <ArticleTitle>Diagnostic performance of neck circumference to identify overweight and obesity as defined by body mass index in children and adolescents: systematic review and meta-analysis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>223-229</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1080/03014460.2016.1224387</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The neck circumference (NC) has been shown to be an accurate index for screening overweight and obesity in children and adolescents.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To perform a meta-analysis to assess the performance of NC for the assessment of overweight and obesity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Data sources were PubMed and EMBASE up to March 2016. Studies providing measures of diagnostic performance of NC and using body mass index as reference standard were included.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Six eligible studies that evaluated 11 214 children and adolescents aged 6-18 years were included in the meta-analysis. NC showed pooled sensitivity to detect high body mass index of 0.780 (95% confidence interval [CI] = 0.765-0.794), specificity of 0.746 (95% CI =  0.736-0.756) and a diagnostic odds ratio of 17.343 (95% CI =  8.743-34.405).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The NC had moderate diagnostic accuracy for identifying overweight and obesity in children and adolescents.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
